Using a script (I'm using javascript as I am not familiar with lua or its syntax) it is possible to associate a property as a vo_gpu_next shader input using mp.observe_property. But there still is a problem here as playback-time is initially undefined when the file loads.
Obviously it is possible to test the value in the function before passing it on to the shader and the script approach can work, but this does end up being more complex than is desirable.

```
//obs.js:
mp.observe_property("playback-time", "number", play_time);
function play_time(name, value) {
	print (value);
	mp.command("no-osd set glsl-shader-opts time_s=" +value);
}
```

> I'm using javascript as I am not familiar with lua or its syntax

I'm not happy with lua too. but mujs only supports ES5 and the perf much worse than lua-jit.

You can use this: https://typescripttolua.github.io/, to avoid lots of traps of lua.

> more complex than is desirable

These checks was enough I think.

```js
mp.observe_property("playback-time", "native", (name, value) => {
    if (typeof value !== "number" || Number.isNaN(value))
        return;

    // ...
});
```

**Edit:**
I don't know this value could changes how frequently and how fast the shader can apply the change, you may need to add a debounce.
